  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nSouthwesterly low level flow ahead of a cold front northwest of the \nforecast area today is contributing to a seasonably warm day. \nTemperatures in the 70s are actually approaching records, while \naverage highs are in the upper 50s this time of the year. The \nboundary north of the area today moves to around I-70 tonight and \nbecomes more diffuse. Nearly calm winds and clear skies create \nfavorable conditions for radiational cooling overnight, which will \ntake temperatures back into the 40s, below our crossover temperature.\nThus, at least patchy fog, especially in river valleys or near water\nbodies seems probably tonight/early Tuesday. Temperatures tomorrow \nwill be slightly cooler than today but still well above average.\n\nA stronger cold front will push southward through the area early \nWednesday as an upper trough quickly dives out of the Northern High \nPlains. No precipitation is expected with this system. Northwest \nwinds will be gusting 20 to 30 mph behind the boundary as cooler air \nfilters into the area. Temperatures in the 60s will still feel nice,\nbut the breeze will be a factor. Much cooler conditions can then be \nexpected for Thursday, and especially by Friday behind a secondary \ntrough axis aloft and as a sfc ridge builds in from the north.\n\nTemperatures begin to rebound this weekend as the sfc ridge moves \neast and an H5 ridge builds aloft. POPs then increase by Sunday \nafternoon as our next system approaches. Most models track the \nsystem to our south, but an initial area of enhanced lift within a \nrapidly moistening atmosphere ahead of the H5 trough could result in \na batch of rain impacting northeastern Kansas during the day.",
